{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact combined head 0eb5a1aaf4174b728745d331507900f759f64e00, tree 5e915bb08bb434bf43b841e03d8ba1c629135c11, on builder/combined-pg1-h1-lv1-hl1-cuta2-ctl1-20261114, all 76 artifacts, for request f8c8d7cd under my promise 4b42ebfc and the nine implementation promises that the request names. The head carries PG-1, H1, LV1, HL-1 with its three follow-ups, CUT-A2 and CTL-1 onto landed R2 main 189cf7b6. It keeps every new form behind a refusal at the runtime, the bytes check or replay, and it changes no Grow source, packet or deployment.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- PG-1. `predictionGrant` in `packages/derive/src/grant.ts` builds one current grant for every action that `actionOf` finds, with `issued` at sequence 0 and a zero digest, `notAfter` null, and `fresh` set to null by one named cast. No production module imports it: only `packages/derive/test/fixtures.ts` and `prediction-grant.test.ts` call it. The deployed paths still present authority through `judgeGrant` and `grantFrom(use)`, and replay's proven mode refuses a grant whose `issued` names no fact. The documented limits hold as stated: no observation proof, so `observes` rows answer `authority-unavailable`, and the caller's clock decides `expired`.\n- H1. `contract` gains the sequenced act mark, `reads` (at most 4), `GroupRead`, the `read` operand, `Entry.position` and `Entry.reads`, `GroupReadUse`, the six inputs, the retained kind `page`, the `at` read types, the scope kind `order` and `platform:order`. Each new form stops before any write or derivation:\n  - `isInput` in `packages/bytes/src/records.ts` refuses all six inputs.\n  - `retainedByteDomain` throws for `page`, and the `RETAINED` table excludes it.\n  - `operand.ts` and `read-value.ts` throw for a `read` operand.\n  - `derivable` is false for the sequenced and live levels, so `Scope` answers `unsupported-definition` through `#derives` (`packages/scope/src/core.ts:382`).\n  - Replay stops with `unsupported-definition` at any of the six inputs.\n  - No runtime module names `order` or `platform:order`.\n  The validator admits `sequenced` and `reads` only on an act of a sequenced definition, refuses `staleness`, refuses a `create` send from a sequenced act, refuses both marks on a handler, and admits a `read` operand only as the `list` of `each`, `has`, `distinct` or `sameSet`.\n- LV1. The validator admits live version 1 and refuses capabilities, fact field types, `presents`, handlers, declared sends, outcome sends and notifications. A platform declaration that states live is refused `live-platform`. `applyEntry` refuses every entry after a live scope becomes active, before any mutation.\n- The amended sequenced and live notes match the code: the `read` operand without `part`, the reason table under ruling `09d80b57` with the head that adds each code, and the live platform limit.\n- HL-1. `scripts/test.mjs` exports `nodeFiles`, enrols `j5`, the two-process owner and `test-selection.test.mjs`, and lists six exclusions, each with a reason. The two-process fixture awaits both child closes before it closes HTTP and removes its home. The head deletes `scripts/demo-git-host.mjs`, its test and the two unused browser witness modules. The only remaining references are in the frozen fixture `version-questions-86179b0c.json`, as the note says.\n- CUT-A2. `registerValuesMain` refuses neither, both or a repeated flag. `registerValuesSource` refuses a missing or invalid `mode` and the legacy `only` and `keep` fields. The write path checks that the target is a regular file, stages the candidate with the observed mode, and checks device, inode, mode and bytes again before the rename. The note and `docs/deploy.md` state that the file must stay idle.\n- CTL-1. `scripts/control.mjs` classifies an assertion from the complete first line before `firstLine` cuts it at 300 characters. It matches only `AssertionError` and the two whole-line promise forms of the pinned Vitest. A timeout is never an assertion.\n- The seals. `security-entry-points.test.mjs` changes only the `packages/bytes/src/records.ts` row. I reviewed that diff above. The C1 and R2 rows stay as main has them.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 189cf7b6 is an ancestor, and the pushed branch equals the head. All 76 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --name-only 189cf7b6 0eb5a1aa`, including the four deletions.\n- Gate, under f45b355a and act 13ea7ab7. `gate.log` in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-14-combined-batch-gate-attempt2` has SHA-256 84a48c91…, as the note states. Its first lines name head 1c80cef3 and tree 9ed79ccf with 0 changed files, and its last line is \"gate passed\". The five CUT-A2 control files beside it match the note's digests. From 1c80cef3 to the head, only the combined note changes, so the documentation-only follow-up rule applies.\n- Generated output and identifiers. The gate at 1c80cef3 ran the asset comparison, MCP's generated-module freshness owner and the R1 reproduction owner, and all three passed. The head changes no source after that gate. I ran no generator.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 28 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Each level enters as a validated form with a refusal at the first place that would act on it, so the later heads H2 to H4 and LV2 can add behavior one boundary at a time. `predictionGrant` lives beside the judge whose `Grant` reading it must match.\n\nOne boundary for the record. Rules install and activation validate a definition without `derivable` (`application-install.ts:103`, `definition-input.ts:39`, `rules-scope.ts:660`). From this head on, a room can install or activate an application whose definition is live or sequenced version 1. Creation of its scope then answers `unsupported-definition`, which the Native live-creation witness shows for live with unchanged rows. Nothing is written that a later head cannot use. The planner can decide whether install should refuse a level that no runtime derives, before an author meets that answer.\n\nSecurity: no finding. No new form reaches a write. `predictionGrant` grants every declared action, so it must never reach a write path. Today no production module imports it, and replay refuses an entry that records it. A later head that imports it outside a prediction path should get a security-entry seal for that path. CUT-A2 makes the authority choice explicit: `--keep` widens the installed roots, and a caller must now ask for it. CTL-1 adds only the two pinned promise forms to what counts as an assertion.\n\nSimplification: no required change. `excludedTests` with a reason for each file replaces silent omissions, and the deletions remove unsupported code.\n\nKernel:\n\n- PG-1 adds two exports to `packages/derive`, `predictionGrant` and `PredictionActor`. The PG-1 note gives the section 7 review: the stand-in must match how the judge reads `Grant` and `fresh`. It adds no form that section 7.1 lists and closes no K item.\n- H1 adds the scope kind `order`, the platform name `platform:order`, six entry kinds, the retained kind `page`, the `at` read types and the `read` operand. Ruling `08c5ff25` releases the first version of the sequenced level from the hold of section 7.1. The head adds `order` to K4's list, as section 7.1 says.\n- LV1 adds the validator's admission of live version 1 and the fold's refusal of a later entry, as ruling `4b5e0ec2` permits. It adds nothing to K1's count or K4's list.\n- Section 7.2: the definition corpus gains the sequenced and live data and variant rows, so each admitted form and each refusal has a named row.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing, push, deployment or publication. The planner decides the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:d22e49c0451988a192f1acf5cc7a8883779c7dcc","binding":"assigned","head":"0eb5a1aaf4174b728745d331507900f759f64e00","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1